My guess is what is going on here is that `struct sockaddr_un` is massive due to how NonStop decided to implement it.

TLDR: `struct sockaddr` (well, `struct sockaddr_storage`) is a tagged union structure. This means it needs to have a fixed maximum size. This is a problem with UNIX domain sockets because they are identified by paths. As I understand it Linux basically cheesed a solution to this problem by defining a fixed maximum length for UNIX domain socket paths:

```c
struct sockaddr_un {
	sa_family_t sun_family;
	char sun_path[108];
};
```

So while Linux supports long path names in most cases, for UNIX domains sockets specifically you have a special limitation in order to keep the size of `struct sockaddr_storage` reasonable.

My guess is NonStop decided they didn't care about memory usage and to choose some massive size for `sockaddr_un`, say 2k.

The obvious solution here is to base our testing on the platform's sizeof(BIO_ADDR).

Another option is to avoid using BIO_ADDR here as we never actually use UNIX domain sockets. We could just define a union of `struct sockaddr_in` and `struct sockaddr_in6`. Probably a better option but more work and more duplication. Probably not worth us doing this for NonStop but a PR would probably be accepted if the memory wastage is considered an issue.

